<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <!--
      The starting window, and nothing else.

      Everything the app draws takes its colour from `Nocturne`, which now holds
      two palettes; this style paints only the frame the system puts up before the
      process has drawn anything, and `MainActivity` repaints the window as soon
      as it knows which theme the shop chose. A light-theme shop therefore sees
      the dark ground for the length of a cold start and no longer — the only way
      to remove even that would be to read the shop file before the first frame.

      The bar icons are set at runtime too, for the same reason: white glyphs
      disappear into a white page.

      **There is no bar colour here, and there cannot be.** `statusBarColor` and
      `navigationBarColor` were set to the same ground as the window and were
      silently ignored: Android stopped honouring both at `targetSdk` 35, and this
      app targets 36. The bars are transparent, the app draws underneath them —
      that is what `enableEdgeToEdge` in `MainActivity` asks for — and what shows
      through is whatever pane is behind, which is why every screen pads itself
      with `statusBarsPadding`. Setting them again would look like it worked,
      because the colour matched the window anyway.

      `windowLightStatusBar` is a different attribute and is still obeyed. It
      decides the glyphs on that first frame, before the runtime code above knows
      which theme the shop chose.
    -->
    <style name="Theme.Stockbook" parent="android:Theme.Material.NoActionBar">
        <item name="android:windowBackground">#161826</item>
        <item name="android:windowLightStatusBar">false</item>
    </style>
</resources>
